# Two-service walkthrough: a token issued by the ISSUER (:5199) is spent at
# this separate VERIFIER (:5299) — different process, no shared code, only
# public keys in common. Runnable from Visual Studio 2022 or VS Code
# (REST Client extension).
#
# Start both apps first:
#   Terminal 1:  ASPNETCORE_URLS=http://localhost:5199 dotnet run --project samples/PostQuantum.Identity.Demo
#   Terminal 2:  dotnet run --project samples/PostQuantum.Identity.Verifier.Demo

@issuer   = http://localhost:5199
@verifier = http://localhost:5299

### Verifier info — which kids it trusts and where the keys came from.
GET {{verifier}}/

### 1. Register at the ISSUER.
POST {{issuer}}/register
Content-Type: application/json

{ "username": "ada", "password": "Lovelace#1843" }

### 2. Login at the ISSUER — returns an ML-DSA-65–signed PQ-JWT.
# @name login
POST {{issuer}}/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "username": "ada", "password": "Lovelace#1843" }

### 3. Spend the token at the VERIFIER — crosses the service boundary.
GET {{verifier}}/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.$.token}}

### Negative: no token -> 401 (the handler never reaches business logic).
GET {{verifier}}/orders

### Negative: tampered token -> 401 (signature check fails, fail-closed).
GET {{verifier}}/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.$.token}}tampered

### Honest edge, demonstrated: revoke at the issuer...
POST {{issuer}}/logout
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.$.token}}

### ...and the VERIFIER still accepts it (200) until expiry — revocation does
### not cross service boundaries without a shared store. See the README.
GET {{verifier}}/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.$.token}}
